{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"INDUCED-VOTER_ERROR DRILL — METHOD PRE-REGISTERED BEFORE EXECUTION, so the induction cannot be rationalised after the fact. The criterion requires the method to be stated; stating it in advance is stronger.\n\nWHY NOT THE OBVIOUS ROUTES.\n* A CLOSED CHANGE is explicitly wrong: per [rebar:c943-1b38-d613-4013] a 409 'change is closed' deliberately does NOT emit VOTER_ERROR (voter.py:661-670 records the vote and returns 'skipped'). The criterion says so too.\n* /rerun CANNOT REACH THE CHEAPEST SITE. app.py:293 looks the change up via GerritClient.get_change_event BEFORE enqueuing and returns 502/404 on failure, so a bad id never reaches the voter. And app.py:317 sets event['_rebar_force'] = True, which makes voter.py:456 SKIP the has_llm_review_vote dedup check entirely — so the :470 error site is unreachable on a forced rerun by construction.\n* AN LLM OUTAGE (e.g. blanking the Anthropic key) is a poor instrument: an adapter failure produces a decision with a retryable gap_reason, which DEFERS with no VOTER_ERROR (voter.py:601-632) until the per-revision attempt budget is spent, and only then emits one at :637 — while ALSO casting a real -1 on a real change. Multiple cycles, real spend, and a false blocking vote on someone's change.\n\nTHE CHOSEN INDUCTION — the dedup-check transport failure at voter.py:470, reached through the REAL webhook path.\nMEASURED PREREQUISITE: the bot addresses Gerrit at gerrit_base_url = http://gerrit:8080 — the compose SERVICE NAME on the internal network, not the public origin. And gerrit_client.py:116 wraps a transport failure as GerritError('... transport error: ...'), so a refused connection arrives as the same typed error a 5xx would.\nSo: append '127.0.0.1 gerrit' to /etc/hosts INSIDE the review-bot container. Outbound calls to http://gerrit:8080 then hit the container's own loopback, where nothing listens, and the FIRST Gerrit call in review_and_vote — the has_llm_review_vote dedup check at :456, which the webhook path DOES execute because no force marker is present — raises GerritError and fires _voter_error at :470.\n\nWHY THIS IS THE NARROWEST AVAILABLE INSTRUMENT:\n* ZERO LLM SPEND. The failure precedes any adapter call: the return is {'status':'error','stage':'dedup_check'}.\n* NO VOTE IS CAST, and none can be — the code path returns before a decision exists, so there is no risk of a false +1 or a false -1 on a real change.\n* NOTHING PERSISTENT IS EDITED. No config, no .env, no compose file, no Gerrit ACL, no IAM. /etc/hosts inside a running container is reverted by deleting one line, and is discarded anyway if autodeploy recreates the container — so the blast radius self-heals rather than needing cleanup to be remembered.\n* IT EXERCISES THE WHOLE CHAIN AUTHENTICALLY, from the bot's MAIN process. That matters: observability.sh section 4 greps 'journalctl CONTAINER_NAME=<voter container>', so a marker written by a 'docker compose exec' process would NOT be captured — an exec'd probe is not a substitute for a real one, and would have silently proved nothing.\n\nBLAST RADIUS, stated plainly: while the hosts entry is in place the bot cannot vote on ANY change, so a landing during the window gets no LLM-Review vote. That is recoverable with /rerun and is inherent to the criterion — it asks for the gate to fail once, on purpose. The window will be minutes, and will not overlap a change of mine that is awaiting review.\n\nTIMING, from measurement rather than assumption: the observability probe is a systemd timer with OnUnitActiveSec=5min (rebar-observability.timer, confirmed on the host), and rebar-gerrit-voter-errors is period=300 / evaluation_periods=1 / threshold=0. So a single VOTER_ERROR should reach ALARM within roughly two probe intervals, and 'systemctl start rebar-observability.service' can force the publish immediately rather than waiting.\n\nSEQUENCE: record the baseline alarm state and the current VOTER_ERROR count -> add the hosts entry -> trigger a real review by pushing a change -> confirm the VOTER_ERROR marker in the CONTAINER journal -> force the probe -> observe OK -> ALARM with timestamps -> remove the hosts entry -> /rerun and confirm a normal vote -> observe the alarm return to OK. The retry-after-restore observation doubles as evidence for the denied-access criterion's fourth clause."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785798162703912001,"uuid":"5fbb4807-fa89-4171-83dd-8bd4463a24c8"}